  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nThough ridging aloft will begin to weaken during the back half of\nthe week, low-lvl thermal profiles will lag behind a bit as 850mb\ncontinue to rise toward 26-27C into Thursday. This will result in\nsfc highs reaching 102-108 degrees across the western deserts, \nagain nearing daily records in Yuma and the Imperial Valley. Major\nHeatRisk is expected to be most expansive on Thursday afternoon, \nleading to a spatial extension of the Extreme Heat Warning to \ninclude the entire Lower Colorado River Valley and a portion of \nthe Gila River Valley in Yuma County including Dateland and Tacna.\nPortions of south-central AZ will also see highs climb to around \n100-103 or around 10 degrees above normal on Thursday, although \nthe HeatRisk is expected to remain in the Moderate category. \nFriday will be a transition day where a deepening trough over the \nPac NW which in turn induces SW flow aloft over the West Coast. \nAlthough we will begin to see a reduction in overall hghts aloft \non Friday afternoon, low-lvl thermal profiles will be slow to \nrespond as 850 mb temperatures are still expected to reach 25-26C \nacross the much of the forecast area equating to sfc highs around \n100-105 degrees in most lower desert communities. Due to these\ntemperatures and lingering Major HeatRisk for the Imperial Valley\nand Yuma area, the Extreme Heat Warning will continue through\nFriday.\n\nWe then turn our attention to an upcoming pattern shift heading \ninto this weekend as most deterministic models and ensemble \nmembers are trending toward an overall troffing pattern setting up\nacross the western CONUS. There is now better model agreement \nthat a stronger and deeper trough will transition into a cutoff \nlow over the Pac NW on Saturday and gradually sink southward into \nCA Sunday and Monday. Meanwhile, latest forecast guidance from the\nNHC shows an eventual turn to the northeast by Rachel this \nweekend with the remnants of the storm itself being pulled \nnortheastward and phasing with the west coast trough. As this \npattern transpires, there should be an increase in southwesterly \nflow aloft over the Desert Southwest which will begin to pull \nPacific moisture into the region by late Saturday into Sunday. \nCoincidentally, rain chances will return to the much of the region\nwith NBM PoPs now around 20-50% across south-central AZ on Sunday\nand even up to 40-60% across the foothills NE of Phoenix by \nMonday. There is still high uncertainty in rainfall amounts at \nthis time but there is at least a chance of some higher totals \nexceeding 1.50\" across AZ high terrain. As 500 mb hghts begin to \nlower ahead of the approaching trough along with increasing cloud \ncover and moisture, high temperatures will take a dive from the \nmid 90s on Saturday to the low to mid 80s by Monday.",
